  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1018 PM CST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nLooking toward the extended, part of the aforementioned Great Basin \nlow will become absorbed into the Canadian low whilst the southern \nportion will dig into the Four Corners by 12Z/Sat.  This system will \nremain progressive, transitioning WTX late Saturday evening which, \nin turn, will have driven a front into the CWFA either late Saturday \nafternoon or early evening. Being a vigorous front, some convection \ncould develop along the boundary, but this would typically be \nlimited to the eastern column or so of our counties.  With near-\nzonal flow in the mid levels after the low scoots through, the \nnortherly winds associated with the front will only last about 24 \nhours before return flow sets up again.  The flow remains quite \nprogressive with another system, with negative tilt orientation \nmoving through Monday night.  Looking ahead to Thanksgiving, there's \nquite a bit of variance.  The consistent item is cooler temperatures \nagain, the inconsistent is the prospect for precipitation.  The \nECMAI guidance has been portraying a dry Thanksgiving for a while \nwhile the GFS is more prone to thinking light precipitation might be \nin store...with the cool temps.  Something to look forward about.\n\nTemperatures Friday and Saturday will be pleasantly warm with highs \nlikely near 70 and some lower RH values which will be welcomed by \nthe ag community.   Saturday's cold front will drop temps back into \nthe 50s to lower 60s and that trend appears to head slowly cooler \nthrough the remainder of next week.  A few of the storms Saturday \nafternoon and evening could become strong to possibly severe with \n~500J/kg of CAPE combined with about 40KT of shear.  The shear would \ntend to limit much development given the meager CAPE, but we should \nget greater clarity with additional cohesive data.",
